<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jang Yeong-sil</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1390–after 1442</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jang Yeong-sil was a Korean engineer and inventor of the early Joseon dynasty. Born of low status, he was promoted to high office by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejong-the-great">King Sejong</a> for his skill. He built astronomical instruments, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armillary-sphere">armillary spheres</a>, and the self-striking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/water-clock">water clock</a> of 1434, and worked on Sejong's programme of scientific instruments that also produced a standard rain gauge in 1441. He was dismissed in 1442 after a royal palanquin he had made broke.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jang-yeong-sil.jpg" alt="Jang Yeong-sil" width="320" height="453" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Himasaram · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jang_Youngsil_3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>His precise date of birth is unknown; it is recorded only in the genealogy of the Chang family and in the Veritable Records of the Joseon Dynasty. According to these records his father, Chang Seong-hwi, belonged to the eighth generation of the family and was the third of five brothers who had served as ministers in Goryeo; the father was of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a> descent. His mother was a government-registered courtesan, which made him a government-owned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobi">nobi</a>, one of the lowest classes of Joseon society.</p><p>He first developed his skill in metal working in Gyeongsang Province, where he had spent his early life as a man-slave in government employ. His reputation brought him to the royal court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Hanseong</a>, present-day Seoul, where selected commoners displayed their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a> before the king, and Sejong made him a court technician.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His first celestial globe, built after two months of study from incomplete Arabian and Chinese instructions, was of mediocre accuracy. In 1433 he made an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armillary-sphere">armillary sphere</a>, the honcheonui, turned by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/water-wheel">waterwheel</a> so that it showed the positions of the sun, moon and stars by day and night. These instruments, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sundial">sundials</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/water-clock">water clocks</a>, stood around the Gyeonghoeru Pavilion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeongbokgung">Gyeongbok Palace</a>, and in 1442 the court astronomers compiled the Chiljeongsan, a calendar of the courses of the sun, moon and five visible planets.</p><p>After early attempts failed, he travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> to study <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/water-clock">water clocks</a>. His Jagyeokru announced the hour with a bell, gong and drum: floating arrows released iron balls that struck a cymbal and worked levers moving 12 wooden figures. With Ichun, Kimjo and others he made the bronze bowl sundial Angbu-ilgu, engraved with the animals of the Chinese zodiac for those who could not read, and in 1441 the Supyo, a calibrated stone column for measuring water. He is often credited with the rain gauge, but no written record links him to it; Crown Prince Yi Hyang, later King Munjong, is recorded as first proposing the idea.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Sejong also sent him back to Gyeongsang Province to survey its metals for better weapons. His rise from humble origins made some officials jealous. When the sedan chair, or gama, that he had built for Sejong broke during a journey in 1442, he was held responsible; against the king's wishes he was jailed, flogged 80 times and expelled from the palace. Nothing more is recorded of his life. Some have speculated that he died from the flogging or went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chang Yŏngsil” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sejong's reign also produced the rain gauge of 1441, water clocks and astronomical instruments developed with the engineer <b>Jang Yeong-sil</b>, and books printed with improved metal type.</blockquote><a href="/empire/joseon">Joseon · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Baybars, a Kipchak boy sold at the slave market of Sivas, seized the Mamluk throne in 1260; <b>Jang Yeong-sil</b>, born a government nobi, became a court technician under King Sejong; the Athenian slave Pasion became a banker, a metic and finally a citizen, a career his ladder calls…</blockquote><a href="/essays/from-ruler-to-enslaved">From Ruler to Enslaved</a></li><li><blockquote>A few rose by royal favour: <b>Jang Yeong-sil</b>, born a government nobi, became a court technician under King Sejong and built water clocks, and Sukbin Choe, a palace slave who carried water, became a royal consort and mother of King Yeongjo.</blockquote><a href="/societies/joseon">Social Hierarchy of Joseon Korea</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Busan, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-korea">South Korea</a>. Died in Joseon. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.18,129.08&amp;year=1423" aria-label="Busan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7818 892 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Jang Yeong-sil"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8268 1162h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8268 1162h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.18,129.08&amp;year=1423">What was here in 1423? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
